A table can store the cheapest cost to reach every stair. Start with cost `0` at the first stair, then fill the rest from left to right.

Dry run for `heights = [10, 20, 15]`:
- First stair cost is `0`.
- Stair 1 costs `10`.
- Stair 2 can be reached from stair 1 with cost `15`, or directly from stair 0 with cost `5`.
- The answer is `5`.

This version uses a full array, so it is a little more memory-heavy than the rolling version.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int frogJump(vector<int>& heights) {
        int n = heights.size();
        vector<int> dp(n, 0);

        for (int index = 1; index < n; ++index) {
            int oneStep = dp[index - 1]
                    + abs(heights[index] - heights[index - 1]);

            int twoSteps = INT_MAX;
            if (index > 1) {
                twoSteps = dp[index - 2]
                        + abs(heights[index] - heights[index - 2]);
            }

            dp[index] = min(oneStep, twoSteps);
        }

        return dp[n - 1];
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the DP table.

The recursive state only needs the current stair index. Store the result for each index after calculating it once. This keeps the same simple recurrence while avoiding repeated work.

The check for `index > 1` handles the first stair, where a two-stair jump is not possible.

```java
class Solution {
    private int[] memo;

    public int frogJump(int[] heights) {
        memo = new int[heights.length];
        java.util.Arrays.fill(memo, -1);
        return solve(heights, heights.length - 1);
    }

    private int solve(int[] heights, int index) {
        if (index == 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        if (memo[index] != -1) {
            return memo[index];
        }

        int oneStep = solve(heights, index - 1)
                + Math.abs(heights[index] - heights[index - 1]);

        int twoSteps = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        if (index > 1) {
            twoSteps = solve(heights, index - 2)
                    + Math.abs(heights[index] - heights[index - 2]);
        }

        memo[index] = Math.min(oneStep, twoSteps);
        return memo[index];
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`, since each stair is solved once.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the memo array and recursion stack. The input array is not changed.

The stairs can also be viewed as a small weighted graph. Each index is a node, and an edge to the next one or next two indices has weight equal to the height difference. Dijkstra's algorithm then finds the cheapest route to the last index.

A short dry run with `[10, 30, 20]` gives edge costs `20`, `10`, and `10`. The direct jump costs `10`, while going through the middle costs `30`, so the final answer is `10`.

This is more general than needed for this problem, but it is useful when thinking of jumps as weighted edges.

```java
class Solution {
    public int frogJump(int[] heights) {
        int n = heights.length;
        if (n <= 1) {
            return 0;
        }

        int[] distance = new int[n];
        java.util.Arrays.fill(distance, Integer.MAX_VALUE);
        distance[0] = 0;

        java.util.PriorityQueue<int[]> queue = new java.util.PriorityQueue<>(
                (a, b) -> Integer.compare(a[1], b[1])
        );
        queue.offer(new int[] {0, 0});

        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            int[] current = queue.poll();
            int index = current[0];
            int cost = current[1];

            if (cost != distance[index]) {
                continue;
            }
            if (index == n - 1) {
                return cost;
            }

            for (int jump = 1; jump <= 2; ++jump) {
                int next = index + jump;
                if (next >= n) {
                    continue;
                }

                int moveCost = Math.abs(heights[next] - heights[index]);
                int newCost = cost + moveCost;

                if (newCost < distance[next]) {
                    distance[next] = newCost;
                    queue.offer(new int[] {next, newCost});
                }
            }
        }

        return distance[n - 1];
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` because the priority queue stores stair states.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the distance array and priority queue.

A direct recursive version works by trying both legal moves from each stair. From stair `i`, the frog can go to `i + 1` or `i + 2`, and the cost of a move is the height difference.

The answer for a stair is the smaller cost from those two choices. This is easy to follow, but the same stair is solved many times.

```python
class Solution:
    def frogJump(self, heights):
        n = len(heights)

        def solve(index):
            if index == 0:
                return 0

            one_step = solve(index - 1) + abs(heights[index] - heights[index - 1])
            two_steps = float("inf")

            if index > 1:
                two_steps = solve(index - 2) + abs(heights[index] - heights[index - 2])

            return min(one_step, two_steps)

        return solve(n - 1)
```

Time complexity: `O(2^n)` because many states are calculated again.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the recursion stack.

