## Approach
Define solve(i) = minimum energy needed to reach the last step starting from index i.
If already at the last step, return 0.
Try the 1-step jump.
If possible, try the 2-step jump.
Return the smaller cost.
Store the result in dp[i].

## Solution
```Java
class Solution {
    public int frogJump(int[] heights) {
        int n = heights.length;
        int[] dp = new int[n+1];
        Arrays.fill(dp,-1);
        return solve(0,n,heights,dp);
    }
    public int solve(int i , int n,int[] nums,int[] dp){
        if(i==n-1) return dp[i] =0;
        if(dp[i]!=-1) return dp[i];
        int left = Math.abs(nums[i]-nums[i+1])+solve(i+1,n,nums,dp);
        int Sleft = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        if(i+2<n){
            Sleft = Math.abs(nums[i]-nums[i+2]) + solve(i+2,n,nums,dp);
        }
        return dp[i]= Math.min(left,Sleft);
    }
}
```

